WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:04.910
Before we move on from exponents, we want to just talk about a couple of other exponent rules that

00:04.910 --> 00:06.020
we want to be aware of.

00:06.050 --> 00:12.800
The first is this negative exponent rule and it tells us that when we have a negative exponent, so

00:12.800 --> 00:15.830
we have some base A and then the exponent is negative.

00:15.860 --> 00:23.810
M that we can rewrite that expression without the negative exponent, we can change the exponent from

00:23.810 --> 00:31.100
negative to positive simply by moving this term, this expression into the denominator.

00:31.100 --> 00:34.100
Or we can think about this as taking the reciprocal.

00:34.130 --> 00:41.300
Of course, the implication of this is the opposite situation where if we have the negative exponent

00:41.300 --> 00:46.730
in the denominator, so we have a to the negative m in the denominator, we can rewrite that.

00:46.730 --> 00:53.540
We can rewrite one divided by A to the negative M as just a to the positive M So this is just giving

00:53.540 --> 00:57.410
us a way to move back and forth between a negative and a positive exponent.

00:57.410 --> 01:03.240
So as an example, let's say that we're given two to the power of negative five.

01:03.270 --> 01:06.600
So two is the base, negative five is the exponent.

01:06.600 --> 01:09.900
And we want to simplify this expression.

01:09.900 --> 01:10.560
How do we do it?

01:10.560 --> 01:15.720
Well, the first thing we want to do is change that negative exponent into a positive exponent.

01:15.720 --> 01:18.960
And in order to do that, we can use this rule here.

01:18.960 --> 01:26.790
So we know that this expression is equivalent to one divided by and now two to the positive five instead

01:26.790 --> 01:28.290
of two to the negative five.

01:28.290 --> 01:33.060
And if we simplify two to the five, we know that two to the five is equal to 32.

01:33.090 --> 01:37.140
So this is just one over 32.

01:37.140 --> 01:42.660
Or we could say the value of two to the negative five is the fraction one over 32.

01:42.690 --> 01:45.390
These two expressions are exactly equivalent.

01:45.390 --> 01:46.590
So here we have a fraction.

01:46.590 --> 01:48.480
Here we have a term with an exponent.

01:48.510 --> 01:51.590
These two values are exactly equal.

01:51.600 --> 01:56.580
Let's look at another example, this time with the negative exponent in the denominator.

01:56.580 --> 02:00.480
So here we have a couple of ways of going about this.

02:00.510 --> 02:01.590
We could.

02:02.080 --> 02:09.130
Use this rule to move the term with the negative exponent into the numerator so the x cubed would stay

02:09.130 --> 02:14.710
exactly where it is, but the x to the negative two would move to the numerator to join the x cubed

02:15.040 --> 02:17.680
and it would become x to the positive two.

02:17.680 --> 02:23.560
And then we could say that this is equal to x to the five by the product rule for exponents where the

02:23.560 --> 02:25.990
base stays the same and we add the exponents.

02:26.020 --> 02:31.630
Alternatively, instead of using a negative exponent rule here, we could just use the quotient rule

02:31.660 --> 02:32.830
to rewrite this.

02:32.860 --> 02:35.680
Remember all these exponent rules work together.

02:35.680 --> 02:41.590
So the quotient rule would just simply tell us that we can subtract the exponent in the denominator

02:41.590 --> 02:43.420
from the exponent in the numerator.

02:43.420 --> 02:46.720
So we would get X to the three minus.

02:47.390 --> 02:48.290
Negative two.

02:48.320 --> 02:52.730
Well, three minus negative two is the same as three plus two.

02:52.760 --> 02:56.060
So we would get X to the three plus two or.

02:56.870 --> 02:59.090
X to the five, which we already saw is the case.

02:59.090 --> 03:04.130
So there's multiple ways to go about simplifying this expression here.

03:04.130 --> 03:08.240
We used quotient rule, which we had already learned and we didn't touch the negative exponent rule

03:08.240 --> 03:08.900
at all.

03:08.900 --> 03:13.610
But in this first method here, we applied the negative exponent rule we also have.

03:14.240 --> 03:15.680
Another exponent rule here.

03:15.680 --> 03:20.780
It tells us that if we have a fraction and we have a term in the numerator, a term in the denominator,

03:20.780 --> 03:24.320
and they're both raised to the same exponent, to the same power.

03:24.320 --> 03:27.680
So here we have a raised to the power of N and B raised to the power of N.

03:27.680 --> 03:30.470
So those exponents are equal to each other.

03:30.500 --> 03:36.710
Then we can rewrite that fraction and we can just express the fraction as a divided by B, just the

03:36.710 --> 03:40.550
fraction of the bases and move the exponent out here.

03:40.550 --> 03:48.140
So another example of this might be this fraction here we have X raised to the negative five power.

03:48.140 --> 03:50.600
We have Y raised to the negative five power.

03:50.600 --> 03:56.540
So those exponents are the same, which means that we can pull that exponent out.

03:56.570 --> 04:06.590
We can just take the fraction of the bases so just X divided by Y and move the exponent outside of the

04:06.590 --> 04:07.130
parentheses.

04:07.130 --> 04:11.390
So this is X divided by y'all raised to the negative five power.

04:11.390 --> 04:14.280
That's what this exponent rule tells us We can do.

04:14.280 --> 04:16.620
But then we have another exponent rule.

04:16.620 --> 04:22.410
This one here, we sometimes call this the reciprocal rule, but it tells us that when we have some

04:22.410 --> 04:28.680
fraction raised to a negative exponent, that that's equivalent to the reciprocal of the fraction raised

04:28.680 --> 04:32.310
to the opposite of that exponent or that positive exponent.

04:32.310 --> 04:34.260
So that's the situation we have here.

04:34.260 --> 04:37.440
We have X divided by Y raised to the negative five power.

04:37.680 --> 04:42.060
This rule tells us that we can just take the reciprocal of the fraction.

04:42.060 --> 04:48.690
So instead of x divided by Y, we'll rewrite this as y divided by x.

04:48.690 --> 04:49.860
Just take the reciprocal.

04:49.860 --> 04:54.900
Just flip the fraction upside down and doing so allows us to change the negative exponent.

04:55.410 --> 05:01.140
Into a positive exponent, which means that this original expression, this original fraction can be

05:01.140 --> 05:03.270
rewritten as Y divided by x.

05:03.300 --> 05:05.250
All to the power of five.

05:05.280 --> 05:07.280
We also could have gone about that.

05:08.000 --> 05:10.730
This way, taking the steps in a different order.

05:10.730 --> 05:16.880
We could have here applied the negative exponent rule to this fraction first by moving the Y to the

05:16.880 --> 05:21.380
negative five to the numerator to make the exponent positive and moving the X to the negative five to

05:21.380 --> 05:23.560
the denominator to make the exponent positive.

05:23.570 --> 05:30.170
And so our first step could have been y to the five divided by x to the five, and then we could have

05:30.170 --> 05:39.410
applied this rule here, taking the fraction of just the bases so Y divided by x and pulling out that

05:39.410 --> 05:41.690
common exponent like that.

05:41.690 --> 05:43.490
We end up in the same place both ways.

05:43.490 --> 05:45.650
But we took a different set of steps to get there.

05:45.650 --> 05:49.160
And then the very last thing is this idea of the zero exponent rule.

05:49.160 --> 05:50.590
And this is the simplest one.

05:50.600 --> 05:56.330
It just tells us that any nonzero value raised to the zero power, in other words, the exponent is

05:56.330 --> 05:59.600
zero, that that's going to be equal to one.

05:59.600 --> 06:02.660
So whenever we have any quantity, it doesn't matter what it is.

06:02.690 --> 06:05.210
If the exponent is zero, that quantity is going to be one.

06:05.210 --> 06:11.780
So A to the zero is going to be equal to one, two to the zero is going to be equal to one.

06:11.790 --> 06:18.450
If we had Y divided by x here to the zero power, then that would be equal to one.

06:18.450 --> 06:23.760
Anytime we have a zero for the exponent, no matter what the base is, the result is going to be one.

06:23.790 --> 06:29.250
The only thing we have to say is that the base has to be nonzero, otherwise the whole expression is

06:29.250 --> 06:29.850
defined.

06:29.850 --> 06:35.850
So here this is true as long as a itself is not equal to zero.

06:35.880 --> 06:42.300
Here, this is true as long as Y divided by x is not equal to zero, which would just mean that Y is

06:42.300 --> 06:43.320
not equal to zero.

06:43.320 --> 06:47.220
Because the only way to make y divided by x equal to zero is if y is equal to zero.

06:47.220 --> 06:52.230
So this is true as long as y is not zero.

06:52.230 --> 06:56.550
And you could say here for this one that this is true as long as two is not equal to zero.

06:56.550 --> 06:58.080
But of course we know that's true.

06:58.080 --> 07:00.540
So we don't have to state that caveat.

07:00.570 --> 07:05.370
The only reason we do it here is because we don't know exactly what the value of a is here.

07:05.370 --> 07:07.860
We don't know what the value of Y divided by X is.

07:07.860 --> 07:10.200
So we have to indicate that this is true.

07:10.200 --> 07:14.910
The zero exponent rule applies as long as the base is nonzero.

07:14.910 --> 07:20.370
But if we know the base is nonzero, then if the exponent is zero then that expression is going to be

07:20.370 --> 07:21.000
equal to one.

07:21.000 --> 07:22.650
So we have this negative exponent rule.

07:22.650 --> 07:24.510
We have that sort of working both ways.

07:24.510 --> 07:30.960
We kind of have this reciprocal exponent rule and the zero exponent rule together with the rules we

07:30.960 --> 07:36.720
learned about before, which remember were the product quotient and power rules, we can use all of

07:36.720 --> 07:43.860
these together as a library of exponent rules to help us simplify expressions with exponents.
